# Regression test: stream:tee gives each branch its own output stream via
# `branch_sender.initialize(&types)?` (a plain pipe(), not `context.initialize_output`),
# so unlike tee's own passthrough output, a branch's stream never got a control receiver
# wired up (see TableOutputStream::control's and stream:group's own broadcast_control's
# doc comments for why a plain, uncontrolled TableOutputStream can't respond to
# crush:pause/crush:terminate at all). A branch that never reads its own input (here, an
# unconditional `while` loop) fills that stream's bounded row buffer, and tee's own
# row-feeding loop used to block forever on a plain, uncontrolled send trying to feed it
# more.
#
# Fixing that alone isn't enough to observe cleanly, though: a closure's body runs as its
# own nested job (see GlobalState::descendant_job_ids' doc comment), which never used to
# inherit crush:terminate/crush:pause sent to the outer job -- so even with tee's own
# send fixed, this script's process would still hang forever at exit, stuck joining the
# `while` branch's own permanently-running thread. Both fixes landed together: tee's
# branch streams are now wired for control, and GlobalState::terminate (along with
# pause/resume) now also reaches every job nested inside the one it's told to stop.
#
# No fancy assertions needed: if either fix regresses, this script hangs, and
# run_system_test's own 30s timeout fails the test -- exactly the same "let a real hang
# fail the test" approach as join_large_stream_deadlock.crush and
# echo_background_stream_does_not_block.crush. 10,000 rows is comfortably past the row
# channel's buffer, so the `while` branch is provably still being fed (and tee's own loop
# provably still blocked on it) once the background dispatch above returns. The other two
# branches (`count`, `materialize`) exercise the same fan-out across multiple branches at
# once, the same class of bug stream:group had before its own fix (only one of several
# workers ever actually seeing a pause/terminate).

$job_id := $(seq 1 10000 | tee {while {$true} {}} {count} {materialize} | count &)
sleep $(duration:of milliseconds=300)
crush:terminate $job_id
echo "ok"
